---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import Summary from '../../components/Summary.astro';
import Row from '../../components/Row.astro';
import Decision from '../../components/Decision.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import Gallery from '../../components/Gallery.astro';
import Note from '../../components/Note.astro';
---
<Case
  slug="handraw"
  title="Handraw · Product Design Case Study · Beatriz Medina"
  description="How I led design on Handraw, a Mac canvas where the camera turns hand movement into strokes: research, first-release scope, design system and onboarding."
  kicker='<span class="idx">Case 01</span>macOS · 2026'
  lede="A Mac canvas where the camera reads your hand movements and turns them into strokes, with no mouse, trackpad or stylus in between."
  chips={['Design leadership', 'User research', 'MVP scoping', 'Design system']}
  spec={[
    ['Role', 'Lead Designer'],
    ['Team', '5 people at the Apple Developer Academy'],
    ['Design', 'Paired with a developer moving into design'],
    ['Timeline', '7 weeks · macOS'],
    ['Launch', 'July 2026'],
  ]}
  store={{ href: 'https://apps.apple.com/br/app/handraw-canvas-por-gestos/id6786850155?mt=12', label: 'Mac App Store' }}
  cover={{ src: 'handraw/screenshot-2.jpg', alt: 'Handraw on a Mac: blank canvas, open color palette and the camera preview tracking both hands' }}
  toc={[
    { id: 'resumo', label: 'Summary' },
    { id: 'handraw-pesquisa', label: 'Research' },
    { id: 'handraw-ideacao', label: 'Scope' },
    { id: 'handraw-interface', label: 'Interface' },
    { id: 'handraw-validacao', label: 'Onboarding' },
    { id: 'handraw-colaboracao', label: 'Collaboration' },
    { id: 'handraw-resultado', label: 'Outcome' },
    { id: 'artefatos', label: 'Artifacts' },
  ]}
  next={{ href: '/en/sete.html', title: 'SETE', sub: 'iOS + watchOS · 2025' }}
>
  <section id="resumo" aria-labelledby="t-resumo">
    <h2 id="t-resumo" class="label">In 30 seconds</h2>
    <Summary>
      <Row label="Problem">People still reach for paper when they think visually. On a computer, the mouse and trackpad demand precision too early and break the train of thought.</Row>
      <Row label="My role">Lead Designer on a team of 5. I led research, UX/UI and the design system and designed most of the screens, working as a design pair with a developer moving into design.</Row>
      <Row label="Key decision">Prioritize the core of the first release: 6 essential requirements out of 29 mapped.</Row>
      <Row label="Outcome">Released on the Mac App Store in July 2026, under my developer account.</Row>
    </Summary>
    <div class="prose lead-in" id="handraw-estrategia">
      <p>Handraw starts from a simple question: what if drawing on a Mac felt as natural as gesturing an idea? The camera tracks your hands and turns movement into strokes. The design challenge was making that feel obvious to people who had never used anything like it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="handraw-pesquisa" class="block">
    <p class="label">Research · 4 interviews</p>
    <h2>In conversation, hands explain the idea. At the computer, the gesture disappears.</h2>
    <div class="block-grid">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>I interviewed four people who use a Mac every day to study, work and create. I asked how they organize ideas, what they do when their head gets tired and what happens when they try to capture something highly visual.</p>
        <p>The same answer kept coming back: when an idea is visual, the computer gets in the way, and the notebook becomes the first draft again.</p>
      </div>
      <Note label="What changed">The goal was no longer to help people draw better. It became not interrupting their thinking.</Note>
    </div>
    <ul class="quotes">
      <li><blockquote>“Sinto, bastante, não consigo me expressar o que é aquilo que eu tenho em mente”<span class="quote-tr" lang="en">I feel it a lot, I can’t express what I have in mind.</span></blockquote><span>Interview 1</span></li>
      <li><blockquote>“Fazer gráfico é horrível, peço pro chat gerar pra mim, no caderno é rapidinho, eu desenho, copio e jogo pro mac”<span class="quote-tr" lang="en">Making charts is awful, I ask the chat to generate them for me. In my notebook it’s quick: I draw, copy it and drop it into the Mac.</span></blockquote><span>Interview 1</span></li>
      <li><blockquote>“Seria um atalho rápido, ele abre uma telinha rápida que eu posso rabiscar bem rápido, onde eu posso rabiscar livremente.”<span class="quote-tr" lang="en">It would be a quick shortcut: it opens a small window where I can sketch really fast, where I can sketch freely.</span></blockquote><span>Interview 4</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="aside-line">Interviews were conducted in Portuguese. Quotes are kept in the original, with an English translation below each.</p>
    <Figure src="handraw/entrevistas.jpg" alt="Synthesis of interviews with 4 participants, organized in columns by participant" caption="Synthesis of the four interviews (in Portuguese)." n={1} crop />

    <div class="sub-block" id="handraw-personas">
      <h3>Two ways of thinking visually</h3>
      <p class="sub-lede">The personas synthesize the interviews. Catarina thinks in motion and needs room to create; Gabriel explains systems to his team by gesturing. What they share: getting an idea out of their head without a mechanical middleman.</p>
      <Gallery items={[
        { src: 'handraw/persona-1.jpg', alt: 'Persona Catarina, 23, university Design student', caption: 'Catarina, Design student' },
        { src: 'handraw/persona-2.jpg', alt: 'Persona Gabriel, 21, Computer Science student and front-end developer', caption: 'Gabriel, Computer Science student' },
      ]} />
    </div>
    <p class="aside-line">For competitive analysis, we looked at everything from Figma, Miro and FigJam to Procreate, Concepts and Canva. The closest to our idea were Airdraw, Freeform and Paint.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="handraw-ideacao" class="block">
    <p class="label">Decision</p>
    <h2>Prioritize what proves the idea.</h2>
    <Decision n="01" title="First-release scope" id="d1">
      <Row label="Context">7 weeks, and an interaction model most people had never used.</Row>
      <Row label="Options">29 requirements, drawn from the team’s user stories and sorted in an impact/effort matrix as essential, important, polish or sacrifice.</Row>
      <Row label="Choice" choice>The 6 essentials: hand detection, drawing by hand, camera capture zone, gesture guide, visual gesture feedback and undo/redo.</Row>
      <Row label="Left out">Marked as sacrifice: export, layers, image import, sticky notes and cropping.</Row>
      <Row label="Why">If gestures didn’t reliably turn into strokes, no editing feature would save the product. Locking scope early also gave development a clear target.</Row>
      <Figure slot="figure" src="handraw/matriz-esforco-impacto.jpg" alt="Impact/effort matrix with 29 requirements classified as essential, important, polish and sacrifice" caption="Red: essential. Yellow: important. Green: polish. Black: sacrifice." n={2} />
    </Decision>
  </section>

  <section id="handraw-interface" class="block">
    <p class="label">Interface</p>
    <h2>A visual foundation so two people could design as one.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>I set up the design system before the final screens: color, typography, spacing and icons. With that foundation, the pair could split screens without losing consistency, and I designed most of the mid- and high-fidelity interfaces.</p>
    </div>
    <Figure src="handraw/style-guide.jpg" alt="Handraw style guide with palette, typography, spacing and icon set" caption="Style guide: color, typography, spacing and icons." n={3} crop />

    <div class="sub-block" id="handraw-naming">
      <h3>A name that says what the product does</h3>
      <p class="sub-lede">Before naming, we defined purpose, audience and competitors, and placed the brand on a tone scale: clear, innovative, functional, universal and direct. We used AI to explore candidates from those guidelines. Handraw won because it joins the product’s two core ideas: hand and draw.</p>
      <Gallery cols={3} items={[
        { src: 'handraw/naming-3.jpg', alt: 'Brand purpose, positioning, audience and competition questions', caption: 'Purpose and positioning' },
        { src: 'handraw/naming-4.jpg', alt: 'Naming tone scale: clear, innovative, between sophisticated and popular, functional, universal and direct', caption: 'Tone scale' },
        { src: 'handraw/naming-5.jpg', alt: 'Prompt with brand guidelines and the list of generated name candidates', caption: 'Exploring names with AI' },
      ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="handraw-validacao" class="block">
    <p class="label">Onboarding</p>
    <h2>Teaching a gesture almost nobody knows.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Onboarding was mainly owned by my design partner; I followed the decisions and how the visual system was applied. The teaching structure was defined and revised in mid-fidelity before moving to the final version.</p>
      <p>The mid-fidelity guide packed six gestures into a single screen. The final version teaches one step at a time, centered on the open hand and the pinch.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="compare">
      <Figure src="handraw/mid-01-selecionar-ferramenta.jpg" alt="Mid-fidelity onboarding: instruction centered at the top and an open-hand icon" tag="Mid-fidelity" />
      <Figure src="handraw/hi-01-selecione-o-pincel.jpg" alt="High-fidelity onboarding: corner card showing step 1 of 5, a pinch gesture illustration and a next button" tag="High-fidelity" after />
    </div>
    <dl class="changes">
      <Row label="Instruction">More direct and specific: “Selecione o pincel” (Select the brush).</Row>
      <Row label="Gesture">The pinch is illustrated, with contact points marked.</Row>
      <Row label="Progress">“1 de 5” (1 of 5) shows what’s left, and a button moves to the next step.</Row>
      <Row label="Space">The instruction moved out of the center and freed up the canvas.</Row>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section id="handraw-colaboracao" class="block">
    <p class="label">Collaboration</p>
    <h2>Leading also meant teaching.</h2>
    <dl class="split">
      <Row label="Me">Research, UX/UI, design system and most mid- and high-fidelity screens.</Row>
      <Row label="My design partner">Gestures and onboarding, with my guidance.</Row>
      <Row label="Together">Visual identity.</Row>
    </dl>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>My partner came from Swift development and was moving into design. Splitting the work this way gave him ownership of a core part of the product, and gave me the job of keeping research, system and screens consistent between the two of us.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="handraw-resultado" class="block">
    <p class="label">Outcome</p>
    <h2>Released on the Mac App Store in July 2026.</h2>
    <Summary plain>
      <Row label="Shipped">Version 1.0, released under my developer account. According to the store listing, the app lets you draw with your hands, undo and navigate with mid-air gestures, add text by voice and follow a visual gesture guide.</Row>
      <Row label="Beyond the core">Voice text input was one of the important requirements, not an essential one, and still made it into the first release.</Row>
      <Row label="What I learned">Leading also means making room: sharing what I know and helping someone else grow during the project.</Row>
      <Row label="Team">Beatriz Medina, João Schwaab, Linda Marie, Manoel Pedro and Marcos Albuquerque, mentored by Filipe Ribeiro Silva Lopes and Nadja Caldeira Guedes.</Row>
    </Summary>
  </section>

  <section id="artefatos" class="block">
    <p class="label">Process artifacts</p>
    <h2>For those who want to see behind the scenes.</h2>
    <details class="artifacts" id="handraw-historias">
      <summary>User stories</summary>
      <Figure src="handraw/user-stories.jpg" alt="User stories for Catarina and Gabriel" caption="User stories that fed the prioritization matrix (in Portuguese)." />
    </details>
    <details class="artifacts" id="handraw-naming-completo">
      <summary>Full naming process</summary>
      <Gallery cols={3} items={[
        { src: 'handraw/naming-1.jpg', alt: 'Benchmark of app names in the same space', caption: 'Name benchmark' },
        { src: 'handraw/naming-2.jpg', alt: 'Names grouped by type', caption: 'Names grouped by type' },
        { src: 'handraw/naming-6.jpg', alt: 'The chosen name: Handraw', caption: 'The chosen name' },
      ]} />
    </details>
    <details class="artifacts" id="handraw-telas">
      <summary>Icon, tokens and more screens</summary>
      <Gallery cols={3} items={[
        { src: 'handraw/logo.jpg', alt: 'Handraw app icon', caption: 'App icon' },
        { src: 'handraw/design-system.jpg', alt: 'Design system tokens: color, typography, spacing and corner radius', caption: 'Design system tokens' },
        { src: 'handraw/mid-02-gestos.jpg', alt: 'Mid-fidelity canvas with the six-gesture guide open', caption: 'Mid-fidelity: gesture guide' },
        { src: 'handraw/hi-02-desenhe-com-o-pincel.jpg', alt: 'High-fidelity onboarding, step 2 of 5: draw with the brush', caption: 'High-fidelity: step 2 of 5' },
        { src: 'handraw/screenshot-1.jpg', alt: 'Handraw presentation screen, gesture canvas for macOS', caption: 'App Store presentation' },
        { src: 'handraw/screenshot-3.jpg', alt: 'Presentation screen of the gesture guide next to the canvas', caption: 'Gesture guide' },
      ]} />
    </details>
  </section>
</Case>
